<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Table 1: </span>SlimPajama mixes used in <a href="#S2.F2" title="In Experimental setup. ‣ 2 TRECs predict effective data placement ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> placement tests:
General blend is original distribution, code blend is HQ data.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S2.F2.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:262.3pt;">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<div id="S2.F2.fig2.1" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:128.6pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25380v1/place_positions.617M.82tpp.jump1.placement.svg" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:178/175;" width="178" height="175"></object>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<div id="S2.F2.fig2.2" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:128.6pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25380v1/contrast_loss_and_reeval.617M.82tpp.decay10.placement.AB.svg" id="S2.F2.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:178/175;" width="178" height="175"></object>
</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S2.F2.fig2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TRECs predict best placement.</span> <span id="S2.F2.fig2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S2.F2.fig2.8.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Left</em></span>:
Example placement curriculum 5-of-10 for <span id="S2.F2.fig2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S2.F2.fig2.9.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Step</em></span> decay. <span id="S2.F2.fig2.10" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S2.F2.fig2.10.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Right</em></span>:
Results for <math id="S2.F2.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mbox{10}\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>10</mtext><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{10}\times</annotation></semantics></math>.
</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate <a href="#S2" title="2 TRECs predict effective data placement ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we conduct a data placement sweep:
for each LR schedule, we train 10 models, each with the 5B CB tokens
inserted into a different 10% segment of the training data. An
example curriculum is shown in <a href="#S2.F2" title="In Experimental setup. ‣ 2 TRECs predict effective data placement ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">left</em></span>.
Also plotted is the standard <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#EB730C;">training curve</span>
(loss on unseen batches during training). While the <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">train loss</em>
shows a familiar decrease with LR
annealing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tissue et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schaipp et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, note the
<em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">TREC</em> <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">increases</em> after the LR drops.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Results.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Results in <a href="#S0.F1" title="In Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (<span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">left</em></span>, <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Step</em></span> schedule),
<a href="#S2.F2" title="In Experimental setup. ‣ 2 TRECs predict effective data placement ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (<span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">right</em></span>, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mbox{10}\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>10</mtext><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{10}\times</annotation></semantics></math> linear decay), and
appendix <a href="#A4.F11" title="In D.1 Within-schedule placement outcomes ‣ Appendix D Further data placement results ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> (decay-to-zero) show, in all cases,
placing CB data at the step with lowest <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{re}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>re</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{re}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> yields the best
validation loss. Data placement also outperforms uniform training on
AB (appendix <a href="#A4.F12" title="In D.1 Within-schedule placement outcomes ‣ Appendix D Further data placement results ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>).
These results validate our hypothesis within individual training runs,
but note that absolute <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{re}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>re</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{re}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> values do not consistently
generalize <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">across schedules</em> for placement prediction
(<a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 Cross-schedule-TREC placement hypothesis ‣ Appendix D Further data placement results ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="21.77" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 21.77" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,21.77) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#00CC00;" fill="#00CC00" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 15.87 C 0 19.13 2.64 21.77 5.91 21.77 L 544.09 21.77 C 547.36 21.77 550 19.13 550 15.87 L 550 5.91 C 550 2.64 547.36 0 544.09 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#E6FFE6;" fill="#E6FFE6" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 15.87 C 1.97 18.04 3.73 19.8 5.91 19.8 L 544.09 19.8 C 546.27 19.8 548.03 18.04 548.03 15.87 L 548.03 5.91 C 548.03 3.73 546.27 1.97 544.09 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 6.12 7.43)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:38.86em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="537.71"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:38.86em;">
<span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Key takeaway 1</span>: TRECs reliably indicate the best point for high-quality data
insertion.</em></span>
</span></span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>TREC shape is governed by the AdamW timescale</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To apply the insights of <a href="#S2" title="2 TRECs predict effective data placement ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (without having to
train a model twice), we need to predict TRECs in advance. Before
presenting our predictive model (<a href="#S4" title="4 Predicting TRECs: adjusting for training fraction ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), we build
intuition for what controls TRECs (under a fixed LR schedule), finding
they are mainly governed by the AdamW timescale.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Background: the AdamW EMA and its timescale.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">AdamW updates at step <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed in terms of learning rate
<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> and weight decay <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> as:
<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}=(1-\eta\lambda)\theta_{t-1}-\eta\frac{\hat{m}_{t}}{\sqrt{\hat{v}_{t}}+\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>m</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}=(1-\eta\lambda)\theta_{t-1}-\eta\frac{\hat{m}_{t}}{\sqrt{\hat{v}_{t}}+\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{m}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>m</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{m}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{v}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{v}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are bias-corrected
exponentially-weighted moving averages (EMAs) of gradients and squared
gradients, respectively <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma &amp; Ba, 2014</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang &amp; Aitchison (2024)</a></cite> observed that AdamW parameters <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> can also
be viewed as an EMA — of weight <em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">updates</em>. Specifically, the
standard EMA form <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text"><math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}=(1-\alpha)y_{t-1}+\alpha x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}=(1-\alpha)y_{t-1}+\alpha x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
matches AdamW when <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text"><math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}=\theta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}=\theta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text"><math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\eta\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\eta\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span>,
and <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text"><math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}=-\frac{1}{\lambda}\frac{\hat{m}_{t}}{\sqrt{\hat{v}_{t}}+\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>λ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>m</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}=-\frac{1}{\lambda}\frac{\hat{m}_{t}}{\sqrt{\hat{v}_{t}}+\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The <em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">timescale</em> <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nicefrac{{1}}{{\alpha}}=\nicefrac{{1}}{{\eta\lambda}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mpadded voffset="0.3em"><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mpadded><mpadded style="width:-0.15em;" lspace="-0.1em" width="-0.15em"><mo stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo></mpadded><mi mathsize="0.700em">α</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded voffset="0.3em"><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mpadded><mpadded style="width:-0.15em;" lspace="-0.1em" width="-0.15em"><mo stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo></mpadded><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">λ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nicefrac{{1}}{{\alpha}}=\nicefrac{{1}}{{\eta\lambda}}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\mathrm{iter}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>iter</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\mathrm{iter}}</annotation></semantics></math> by
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeauthor"><a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang &amp; Aitchison</a></cite>, represents the approximate number of
iterations over which updates are averaged.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">When expressed in epochs as <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\mathrm{epoch}}=\tau_{\mathrm{iter}}/M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>epoch</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>iter</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\mathrm{epoch}}=\tau_{\mathrm{iter}}/M</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is
the number of iterations per epoch, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citeauthor"><a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang &amp; Aitchison</a></cite> found the
optimal <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\mathrm{epoch}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>epoch</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\mathrm{epoch}}</annotation></semantics></math> remained stable under model and dataset scaling
in image tasks. Maintaining a constant <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\mathrm{epoch}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>epoch</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\mathrm{epoch}}</annotation></semantics></math> requires
decreasing <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> proportionally when <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> increases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Since LLM pre-training typically uses a single epoch, we follow
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dey et al. (2025)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bergsma et al. (2025a)</a></cite> in defining a
normalized timescale <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=\tau_{\mathrm{iter}}/T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>iter</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=\tau_{\mathrm{iter}}/T</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the total
number of optimization steps. As <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=D/B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=D/B</annotation></semantics></math> (total tokens <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> divided
by batch size <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>):</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=\frac{1}{\eta\lambda T}=\frac{B}{\eta\lambda D}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=\frac{1}{\eta\lambda T}=\frac{B}{\eta\lambda D}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="23.52" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 585.43 23.52" width="585.43"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,23.52) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 23.52 L 553.94 23.52 L 553.94 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#E6E6FF;" fill="#E6E6FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 23.52 L 553.94 23.52 L 553.94 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#0000FF;--ltx-fill-color:#0000FF;--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" color="#0000FF" fill="#0000FF" stroke="#0000FF" stroke-width="6.0pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 4.15 23.52 L 4.15 0"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 23.62 7.61)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:37.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="518.48"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:37.47em;">
<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.pic1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.pic1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.pic1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.pic1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hypothesis 2</span>: For a given learning-rate decay schedule (<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.pic1.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text"><em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.pic1.p1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">Linear</em></span>, <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.pic1.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text"><em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.pic1.p1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">Cosine</em></span>, <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.pic1.p1.1.1.4" class="ltx_text"><em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.pic1.p1.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">Step</em></span>,
etc.), the TREC is controlled by the AdamW timescale
<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p5.pic1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.</em></span>
</span></span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In other words, because parameters in AdamW are implicitly weighted
averages over updates (derived from training data), the EMA timescale
<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> governs the scope of data influence on the final model, and
thus the TREC. Yet higher EMA weight alone may not lower TREC loss:
earlier updates can lose influence as our position on the loss
surface <em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">shifts</em> (discussed more in <a href="#S4" title="4 Predicting TRECs: adjusting for training fraction ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training fraction.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Viewing training in terms of discrete optimizer steps becomes limiting
when batch sizes and sequence lengths vary. Instead, we view
optimization as a continuous stochastic process over a fixed dataset,
with different batch sizes yielding different discretizations.
In this perspective, we plot TRECs against <em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">training fraction</em>
<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{t}=t/T=tB/D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{t}=t/T=tB/D</annotation></semantics></math>; this naturally facilitates comparing curves
across different batch sizes, step counts, and dataset sizes.
To reduce noise in small-batch settings (and align with large-batch
models), we also smooth TRECs using a moving-average filter (typically
over a window of 100 steps), smoothing curves without altering the
underlying trajectory.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Results.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We follow the architecture and setup of <a href="#S2" title="2 TRECs predict effective data placement ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, but
apply:

<span id="S3.I1" class="ltx_inline-enumerate">
<span id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(a)</span> <span id="S3.I1.i1.2" class="ltx_text">a linear decay-to-zero LR schedule,
</span></span>
<span id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(b)</span> <span id="S3.I1.i2.2" class="ltx_text">a context length of 2048, and
</span></span>
<span id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item">(c)</span> <span id="S3.I1.i3.2" class="ltx_text">the original GPT2 vocabulary (50257 tokens).
</span></span>
</span>
We train on standard SlimPajama splits with original source weightings.
Plot axes indicate whether the TREC is shown in absolute loss or
normalized (min-max scaled) form.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="S3.F3.2" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:131.2pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25380v1/reevals.617M.80tpp.lrs.paper.svg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:181/178;" width="181" height="178"></object>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:131.2pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25380v1/reevals.617M.80tpp.wds.paper.svg" id="S3.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:181/178;" width="181" height="178"></object>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:131.2pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25380v1/reevals.617M.80tpp.bs.paper.svg" id="S3.F3.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:181/178;" width="181" height="178"></object>
</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S3.F3.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Timescale <math id="S3.F3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> determines TREC shape (610M,
80 TPP).</span> Sweeping <math id="S3.F3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> (<span id="S3.F3.10" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S3.F3.10.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">left</em></span>), <math id="S3.F3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>
(<span id="S3.F3.11" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S3.F3.11.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">middle</em></span>), or <math id="S3.F3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> (<span id="S3.F3.12" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S3.F3.12.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">right</em></span>) produces matching
variations in TRECs when <math id="S3.F3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In Background: the AdamW EMA and its timescale. ‣ 3 TREC shape is governed by the AdamW timescale ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) varies
identically.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S3.F3" title="In Results. ‣ 3 TREC shape is governed by the AdamW timescale ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows normalized TRECs for 610M models
trained to 80 TPP, sweeping <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> in each
subplot. Across hyperparameters, curves with matching <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>
exhibit very similar shapes, reflecting consistent timescale
control. This alignment is especially clear here since all models use
the same ordered training data. Similar patterns hold across other
scales and dataset sizes.
Generally, as the timescale expands (<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> increases), the TREC
minimum (<em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">valley</em>) shifts earlier.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            This shift has a
limit: as <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="footnote1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>), the curves converge
(<a href="#S0.F1" title="In Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <span id="footnote1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="footnote1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">middle</em></span>). This occurs because the EMA
<em id="footnote1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">coefficients</em> (<a href="#S4" title="4 Predicting TRECs: adjusting for training fraction ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) themselves
converge to the shape of the LR schedule (<a href="#A6.SS3" title="F.3 Adam as the limit of AdamW when 𝜆→0 ‣ Appendix F Further prediction results ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.3</span></a>).</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="S3.F4.2" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:131.2pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25380v1/reevals.N.dvaries.tepochs.paper.normed.svg" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:181/166;" width="181" height="166"></object>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="S3.F4.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:131.2pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25380v1/reevals.111M.dvaries.tepochs.paper.svg" id="S3.F4.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:181/166;" width="181" height="166"></object>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="S3.F4.4" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:131.2pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25380v1/reevals.617M.dvaries.tepochs.paper.svg" id="S3.F4.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:181/166;" width="181" height="166"></object>
</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span><span id="S3.F4.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Timescale <math id="S3.F4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and TREC shape across model/dataset scales.</span>
<span id="S3.F4.10" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S3.F4.10.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Left</em></span>: Similar <math id="S3.F4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> yields similar TREC shapes across
model scales when training to 20 TPP (<math id="S3.F4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\approx 0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\approx 0.3</annotation></semantics></math>). At
111M (<span id="S3.F4.11" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S3.F4.11.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">middle</em></span>, <math id="S3.F4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0.021" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.021</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0.021</annotation></semantics></math>) and 610M (<span id="S3.F4.12" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S3.F4.12.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">right</em></span>,
<math id="S3.F4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0.105" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.105</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0.105</annotation></semantics></math>), increasing TPP shifts TRECs slightly
right.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We next examine TREC alignment as models and datasets scale. When
models share the same <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and TPP, their TRECs largely align
(<a href="#S3.F4" title="In Results. ‣ 3 TREC shape is governed by the AdamW timescale ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">left</em></span>), with small <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> differences
causing corresponding shifts. The size of <em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">absolute</em> TREC drops
is also similar across scales when training to the same TPP
(<a href="#S0.F1" title="In Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">right</em></span>). As TPP increases, the TRECs move
slightly right (<a href="#S3.F4" title="In Results. ‣ 3 TREC shape is governed by the AdamW timescale ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.4.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">middle</em></span>, <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.5.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">right</em></span>); e.g.,
at 111M, increasing TPP by <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="64\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>64</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64\times</annotation></semantics></math> moves the TREC valley slightly
later in training. Interestingly, as TPP increases, the size of the
TREC drop diminishes (appendix <a href="#A5.F15" title="In E.1 Further scaling results ‣ Appendix E Further TREC results ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>):
<em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">at lower TPP, training appears to emphasize memorization</em>. This finding aligns
with a recent study of LLM memorization from an information theoretic
perspective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Morris et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, <a href="#A5.SS3" title="E.3 Adam momentum 𝛽_1 and velocity 𝛽_2 ‣ Appendix E Further TREC results ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.3</span></a> shows that large changes in AdamW’s
<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> parameters do not significantly alter TREC
shape (even when <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., no momentum). This suggests the
timescale imposed by weight decay is far more influential than the
timescales of momentum and velocity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p5.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="23.31" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 23.31" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,23.31) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#00CC00;" fill="#00CC00" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 17.4 C 0 20.66 2.64 23.31 5.91 23.31 L 544.09 23.31 C 547.36 23.31 550 20.66 550 17.4 L 550 5.91 C 550 2.64 547.36 0 544.09 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#E6FFE6;" fill="#E6FFE6" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 17.4 C 1.97 19.58 3.73 21.34 5.91 21.34 L 544.09 21.34 C 546.27 21.34 548.03 19.58 548.03 17.4 L 548.03 5.91 C 548.03 3.73 546.27 1.97 544.09 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 6.12 8.2)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:38.86em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="537.71"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p5.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:38.86em;">
<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p5.pic1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p5.pic1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p5.pic1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p5.pic1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Key takeaway 2</span>: Overall, the data broadly supports <a href="#S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" title="Background: the AdamW EMA and its timescale. ‣ 3 TREC shape is governed by the AdamW timescale ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>: the AdamW
timescale (<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p5.pic1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>) predominantly controls TREC shape, with TPP
playing a secondary, smaller role.</em></span>
</span></span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">However, as seen in <a href="#S2.F2" title="In Experimental setup. ‣ 2 TRECs predict effective data placement ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the LR schedule itself
significantly affects TREC shape. To integrate LR schedules into our
analysis, we next adopt an expanded view of the AdamW timescale.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Predicting TRECs: adjusting for training fraction</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following <a href="#S3" title="3 TREC shape is governed by the AdamW timescale ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, one could in principle predict a TREC for
any model by re-evaluating a smaller model with matching <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, TPP,
and LR schedule. More generally, we propose a functional form that
extends the AdamW EMA perspective to time-varying learning rates,
while also incorporating a training-fraction term to
capture <em id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">minimizer drift</em>, described below.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Background: the extended AdamW EMA perspective.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bergsma et al. (2025b)</a></cite> extend <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang &amp; Aitchison (2024)</a></cite> by considering EMAs with time-varying
smoothing parameters <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Setting <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{1}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{1}=1</annotation></semantics></math> (so
<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1}=x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1}=x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>), they show the recursion <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{2}=(1-\alpha_{2})\alpha_{1}x_{1}+\alpha_{2}x_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{2}=(1-\alpha_{2})\alpha_{1}x_{1}+\alpha_{2}x_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and in general:</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}=\sum_{i=1}^{t}\left(\prod_{j=i+1}^{t}(1-\alpha_{j})\right)\alpha_{i}x_{i}=\sum_{i=1}^{t}c_{t,i}x_{i}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}=\sum_{i=1}^{t}\left(\prod_{j=i+1}^{t}(1-\alpha_{j})\right)\alpha_{i}x_{i}=\sum_{i=1}^{t}c_{t,i}x_{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{t,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{t,i}</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies the contribution of input <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to the output <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">With AdamW, the LR/weight decay schedule defines the time-varying
smoothing <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}=\eta_{t}\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}=\eta_{t}\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S3" title="3 TREC shape is governed by the AdamW timescale ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). The EMA
operates over weight
updates <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}=-\frac{1}{\lambda}\frac{\hat{m}_{t}}{\sqrt{\hat{v}_{t}}+\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>λ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>m</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}=-\frac{1}{\lambda}\frac{\hat{m}_{t}}{\sqrt{\hat{v}_{t}}+\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, with larger <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{t,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{t,i}</annotation></semantics></math> indicating the <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th update
contributes more to model weights <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}=\theta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}=\theta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> at step <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
We focus on coefficients for final model weights <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, dropping
subscript <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> for clarity:
<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}=\eta_{i}\lambda\prod_{j=i+1}^{T}(1-\eta_{j}\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}=\eta_{i}\lambda\prod_{j=i+1}^{T}(1-\eta_{j}\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>.
To connect with our continuous-time framing, we interpret <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> over
<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> steps as a continuous function, reparameterizing as
<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\hat{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\hat{t})</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{t}=i/T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{t}=i/T</annotation></semantics></math>, enabling direct comparison
with the TREC <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{re}}(\hat{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>re</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{re}}(\hat{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="S4.F5.2" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:131.2pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25380v1/emas.617M.Cyclic.80tpp.paper.svg" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:181/169;" width="181" height="169"></object>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="S4.F5.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:131.2pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25380v1/preds.617M.Cyclic.80tpp.paper.svg" id="S4.F5.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:181/169;" width="181" height="169"></object>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="S4.F5.4" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:131.2pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25380v1/reevals.617M.Cyclic.80tpp.paper.svg" id="S4.F5.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:181/169;" width="181" height="169"></object>
</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span><span id="S4.F5.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Predicting TRECs (610M, 80 TPP, Cyclic LR).</span>
<span id="S4.F5.10" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S4.F5.10.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Left</em></span>: Normalized EMA coefficients <math id="S4.F5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\hat{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\hat{t})</annotation></semantics></math> in our model.
<span id="S4.F5.11" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S4.F5.11.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Middle</em></span>: Predicted TRECs <math id="S4.F5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{L}}_{\mathrm{re}}(\hat{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>re</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{L}}_{\mathrm{re}}(\hat{t})</annotation></semantics></math> from <a href="#S4.E3" title="In Predicting TREC shape. ‣ 4 Predicting TRECs: adjusting for training fraction ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
<span id="S4.F5.12" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S4.F5.12.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Right</em></span>: True TRECs <math id="S4.F5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{re}}(\hat{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>re</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{re}}(\hat{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Predictions match TREC dips, with early damping and late alignment with (inverted) EMA.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S4.F5" title="In Background: the extended AdamW EMA perspective. ‣ 4 Predicting TRECs: adjusting for training fraction ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> (<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">left</em></span>) shows <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\hat{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\hat{t})</annotation></semantics></math> for a cyclic LR
schedule (schedule shown in <a href="#A6.F19" title="In Experimental setup. ‣ F.2 Schedule history and TRECs: Cyclic vs. WSD ‣ Appendix F Further prediction results ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>). When
<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\hat{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\hat{t})</annotation></semantics></math> drops to zero (i.e., LR is zero), TRECs (<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">right</em></span>)
return to baseline (1.0); when <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\hat{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\hat{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is higher, TRECs dip
lower. Yet <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\hat{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\hat{t})</annotation></semantics></math> influence fades earlier in training,
suggesting it alone does not fully explain TREC shape.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Predicting TREC shape.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">EMA coefficients <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\hat{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\hat{t})</annotation></semantics></math> quantify each update’s contribution
to the final weights, but an update’s <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">effectiveness</em> can fade if
the batch-specific loss surface shifts after the gradient was
computed, i.e., due to <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">minimizer drift</em>.
Motivated by a simplified quadratic analysis
(Appendix <a href="#A10" title="Appendix J Theoretical analysis of TRECs ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J</span></a>), we model drift on
a <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">training-fraction clock</em> that is scale-invariant under <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>P</span>
yet LR-schedule-dependent, similar to the use of <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">normalized
compute</em> in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qiu et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="21.99" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 585.43 21.99" width="585.43"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,21.99) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 21.99 L 553.94 21.99 L 553.94 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#E6E6FF;" fill="#E6E6FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 21.99 L 553.94 21.99 L 553.94 0 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#0000FF;--ltx-fill-color:#0000FF;--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" color="#0000FF" fill="#0000FF" stroke="#0000FF" stroke-width="6.0pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 4.15 21.99 L 4.15 0"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 23.62 6.84)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:37.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="518.48"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:37.47em;">
<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hypothesis 3</span>: TREC shape can be predicted
using the AdamW EMA coefficients combined with an adjustment for
training fraction.</em></span>
</span></span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{re}}(\hat{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>re</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{re}}(\hat{t})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the TREC and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\hat{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\hat{t})</annotation></semantics></math> the EMA
coefficients of the final weights <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, both indexed by
training fraction <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{t}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{t}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. We model the normalized TREC
shape as:</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{L}}_{\mathrm{re}}(\hat{t})=1-c(\hat{t})^{p}\cdot\hat{t}^{m}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>re</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{L}}_{\mathrm{re}}(\hat{t})=1-c(\hat{t})^{p}\cdot\hat{t}^{m},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> are exponents to be fit.
Exponent <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> controls the strength of the EMA contribution, while <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>
(the <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">training-fraction exponent</em>) determines when the predicted
<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{L}}_{\mathrm{re}}(\hat{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>re</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{L}}_{\mathrm{re}}(\hat{t})</annotation></semantics></math> begins to reflect <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\hat{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\hat{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. For
example, with <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=1</annotation></semantics></math>, fluctuations in <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\hat{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\hat{t})</annotation></semantics></math> appear
immediately; with a larger <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{L}}_{\mathrm{re}}(\hat{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>re</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{L}}_{\mathrm{re}}(\hat{t})</annotation></semantics></math> remains near
1 for most of training and only incorporates <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(\hat{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(\hat{t})</annotation></semantics></math> near the
end.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S4.F5" title="In Background: the extended AdamW EMA perspective. ‣ 4 Predicting TRECs: adjusting for training fraction ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> illustrates this formulation: EMA fluctuations
(<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">left</em></span>) are dampened early in training in both predicted
(<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">middle</em></span>, using fits of <a href="#S4.E3" title="In Predicting TREC shape. ‣ 4 Predicting TRECs: adjusting for training fraction ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) and true TRECs
(<span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">right</em></span>).
We focus on predicting <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">shape</em>, not absolute values, and
use <a href="#S4.E3" title="In Predicting TREC shape. ‣ 4 Predicting TRECs: adjusting for training fraction ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> as a normalized functional form. We find
tuning <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> has minor impact on shape prediction compared to <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, so we
fix <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> across experiments and focus on fitting <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Predicting the training-fraction exponent.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a given set of TRECs, we determine the optimal <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> by fitting it
to maximize shape agreement between the predicted and true TRECs. We
use the Pearson correlation <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\mathrm{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\mathrm{p}}</annotation></semantics></math> as a scale- and
shift-invariant measure of this agreement, finding it aligns well with
visual assessments (<a href="#A6.SS1" title="F.1 Further prediction details ‣ Appendix F Further prediction results ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.1</span></a>).
Empirically, we find that optimal exponent <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> closely follows a
power-law relationship with tokens-per-parameter (TPP) and the AdamW
timescale <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, which we express as:</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\ast}=C\cdot(\mathrm{TPP})^{\mu_{1}}\cdot(\tau)^{\mu_{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>TPP</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\ast}=C\cdot(\mathrm{TPP})^{\mu_{1}}\cdot(\tau)^{\mu_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Fitting this power law at a small scale enables us to predict <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>
systematically across model/dataset sizes and hyperparameter settings,
completing the components needed for TREC prediction.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="S4.F6.2" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:131.2pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25380v1/111M.fitting.111M.fitting.cache.dev.svg" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:181/178;" width="181" height="178"></object>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="S4.F6.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:131.2pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25380v1/111M.fitting.617M.fiteval.cache.dev.svg" id="S4.F6.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:181/178;" width="181" height="178"></object>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="S4.F6.4" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:131.2pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.25380v1/111M.fitting.1.7B.fiteval.cache.dev.svg" id="S4.F6.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:181/178;" width="181" height="178"></object>
</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span><span id="S4.F6.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fitted power law for <math id="S4.F6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> aligns with
observed optima.</span>
<span id="S4.F6.10" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S4.F6.10.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Left</em></span>: 111M fit; <span id="S4.F6.11" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S4.F6.11.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">middle</em></span>: 610M eval; <span id="S4.F6.12" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S4.F6.12.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">right</em></span>: 1.7B
eval. Accuracy of 111M fit holds across scale, degrading
slightly for larger models.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Results.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We follow the setup of <a href="#S3" title="3 TREC shape is governed by the AdamW timescale ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, using a <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Linear</em></span> decay-to-zero
LR schedule unless noted. We evaluate prediction accuracy across
scales and datasets using: (i) <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for predicted vs. true
<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> values, and (ii) Pearson <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\mathrm{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\mathrm{p}}</annotation></semantics></math> for predicted vs. true
TREC shape.
We fit the power law <a href="#S4.E4" title="In Predicting the training-fraction exponent. ‣ 4 Predicting TRECs: adjusting for training fraction ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> using <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> values from
small-scale 111M models, trained across varied TPPs and timescales
(<a href="#S4.F6" title="In Predicting the training-fraction exponent. ‣ 4 Predicting TRECs: adjusting for training fraction ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath"><em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">left</em></span>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Fits at 111M generalize to larger scales (<a href="#S4.F6" title="In Predicting the training-fraction exponent. ‣ 4 Predicting TRECs: adjusting for training fraction ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>,
appendix <a href="#A7.F21" title="In G.3 Fits at other model scales ‣ Appendix G Fitting the Training-Fraction Exponent 𝑚^∗ ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>). As shown in
appendix <a href="#A7.T5" title="In G.3 Fits at other model scales ‣ Appendix G Fitting the Training-Fraction Exponent 𝑚^∗ ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, while fit <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> declines from 99%
at 111M to 77% at 3.3B, <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TREC prediction accuracy remains high
across scales (<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\mathrm{p}}\sim 98\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">p</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mn>98</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\mathrm{p}}\sim 98\%</annotation></semantics></math>)</span>, confirming robust predictive
performance even when <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> fits are imperfect.
Ablation results (appendix <a href="#A7.T6" title="In G.3 Fits at other model scales ‣ Appendix G Fitting the Training-Fraction Exponent 𝑚^∗ ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, fitting at 111M,
evaluating at 610M scale) show that both TPP and especially <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>
are important for accurate <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> prediction and TREC shape
matching.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The observed dependence of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and TPP, and also LR
schedule (<a href="#A7.SS4" title="G.4 Fit Generalization Across Learning Rate Schedules ‣ Appendix G Fitting the Training-Fraction Exponent 𝑚^∗ ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.4</span></a>), matches
the <em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">minimizer drift</em> account in <a href="#A10" title="Appendix J Theoretical analysis of TRECs ‣ Predicting Training Re-evaluation Curves Enables Effective Data Curriculums for LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J</span></a>. In the
quadratic view, drift accumulates with the schedule-weighted curvature
<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{\hat{t}}^{1}\eta(s)\,h(s)\,ds" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{\hat{t}}^{1}\eta(s)\,h(s)\,ds</annotation></semantics></math>; shorter EMA timescales
shorten memory and increase drift (larger <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>), higher TPP increases
the extent of curvature evolution and hence drift (larger <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>), and
different LR schedules directly change the cumulative integral,
yielding schedule-specific <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S4.F7.2" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_bottom" style="width:131.2pt;"><span id="S4.F7.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.3pt;">

<span id="S4.F7.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:118.1pt;height:84.6pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-3.5pt,2.5pt) scale(0.944149940038092,0.944149940038092) ;"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.F7.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="124.03" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 159.18 124.03" width="159.18"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,124.03) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(7.42,0) translate(0,37.68)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -1.4 59.99)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 21.76)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 9.61)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_r" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:7.68em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="106.32"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">“CPT” Training </span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 21.76)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_r" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 28.04 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.58em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="49.47"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">Position</span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 96.95 61.33)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 19.06)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 9.61)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_r" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0.4 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.57em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="49.39"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">“CPT” </span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 19.07)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_r" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.71em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="51.35"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">Fraction</span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 8.88 40.32)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:6.68em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="92.48"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.F7.pic1.1" class="ltx_text">5TPP after PT</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -2.81 -6.92)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:8.4em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="116.23"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.F7.pic1.2" class="ltx_text">5TPP from scratch</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#BFBFBF;" fill="#BFBFBF"><path d="M 0 19.69 M 0 19.69 L 0 31.5 L 102.36